  "prompt": "[system]\nYou generate compact online DVRP dispatch policies that minimize TTT (last-truck return time).\n\n[user]\nImplement exactly this interface:\n\ndef choose_next_customer(\n    current_position: np.ndarray,        # (2,) the deciding truck's position\n    depot_position: np.ndarray,          # (2,)\n    truck_positions: np.ndarray,         # (n_trucks, 2) all trucks\n    available_customers: np.ndarray,     # (n_available, 2) revealed unserved customers\n) -> int | None:\n\nRules:\n- DVRP is an online dispatch problem; the objective is to minimize TTT, defined here as the last-truck return time.\n- Decide which customer the active truck (at current_position) should head to next.\n- Return an index into available_customers, or None to wait at the current position.\n- If available_customers is empty, return None.\n- The function is stateless across calls; treat each call as a one-shot decision with the snapshot given.\n- Do not assume coordinates beyond what is passed; do not hard-code instance sizes, truck counts, or dataset details.\n- Do not read/write files, use network, spawn subprocesses, or call external solvers.\n- No module-level mutable globals; the function may be called many times across instances.\n\nSTRATEGY m3_simplify_generalize: Create a simpler and more robust version of one parent by removing brittle, over-specialized, redundant, or overly complex components. Preserve the essential idea and required behavior while improving generalization and maintainability.\n\nUse the provided parents as the primary source of design changes.\n\nIncorporate, adapt, recombine, repair, or refine the listed parents.\n\nDo not ignore the parents or generate an unrelated policy from scratch.\n\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000001: centroid_biased_nearest_neighbor\nStatus: valid; Gap: 53.9486%\nThought: Selects customer minimizing weighted sum of distance from current truck and distance to centroid of all available customers, encouraging movement towards demand clusters. Returns None if no customers.\nMean gap: 53.94857491168691\nMean TTT: 4.508743556671172\nGap by instance size: {'50': 53.94857491168691}\nTTT by instance size: {'50': 4.508743556671172}\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: dvrp:size:50\nHistory role: regime specialist\nCode:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef choose_next_customer(\n    current_position: np.ndarray,\n    depot_position: np.ndarray,\n    truck_positions: np.ndarray,\n    available_customers: np.ndarray,\n) -> int | None:\n    if len(available_customers) == 0:\n        return None\n    centroid = np.mean(available_customers, axis=0)\n    dist_current = np.linalg.norm(available_customers - current_position, axis=1)\n    dist_centroid = np.linalg.norm(available_customers - centroid, axis=1)\n    alpha = 0.3\n    scores = dist_current + alpha * dist_centroid\n    best_idx = int(np.argmin(scores))\n    return best_idx\n```\n\nMinimize TTT: the time until the last truck returns to the depot. This is the only optimization goal.\n\nInternal check before final JSON: correct choose_next_customer signature, returns None or a valid\navailable_customers index, handles empty available_customers no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n{\n  \"name\": \"snake_case_or_title\",\n  \"thought\": \"brief public summary of the dispatch rule, tie-break, and wait condition\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}",
